  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/... \n\nBy Wednesday, a quasi-omega block is shown to develop with an\nanticyclone developing near/over the SoCal coast. Forecast \nconfidence is good that this pattern will hold through the \nend of the workweek. This will result in temperatures remaining \nfairly steady across south-central AZ with afternoon highs in the\n95-100 degree range across the lower deserts and ranging from the\nmid 80s to lower 90s across the higher terrain areas. Meanwhile\ntemperatures are expected to heat up across southeast CA and\nsouthwest AZ with a strengthening of the nearby anticyclone. \nLatest NBM forecast continues to show afternoon highs reaching the\n100-106 degree range across the lower deserts in SE CA and SW AZ \nfor both Wednesday and Thursday. These temperatures will be over \n10 degrees above normal and near daily records in both El Centro, \nCA and Yuma, AZ both days. These temperatures also will likely \nlead to Major HeatRisk for this time of year, with probability of \nMajor HeatRisk at 50-70%. As a result, an Extreme Heat Watch has \nbeen issued for the Yuma area and Imperial Valley for Wednesday \nand Thursday. With high pressure in place dry conditions are \nexpected to persist with no chances for rain, except for near the \nWhite Mtns Wednesday with a weak shortwave moving through the Four\nCorners.\n\nThe high pressure to the west is then expected to start to weaken\nby the end of the workweek and into the weekend with a trough \nmoving into western CONUS this upcoming weekend. However, forecast\nconfidence decreases considerably by the end of the weekend and\nstart of the following week as the ensemble spread is large with \nthe timing of the high weakening and moving out of the region and \nthe trough moving in. The ensembles also disagree on the strength \nof the trough for this upcoming weekend. Either way temperatures \nare expected to gradually decrease back toward normal by this \nupcoming weekend. Additionally, dry conditions look to continue \nthrough at least Saturday, but this could change depending on the\nevolution of troughing during the weekend. Right now better \nchances for rain look to arrive by the beginning of next week, \nwith the best chances across the higher terrain in eastern AZ. \nStay tuned for updates.",
